2) Main characters (short notes)
- Debbie O’Kerry: 16–17, bold, coquettish, impulsive; central protagonist whose vanity and impatience drive important choices (quick marriage, bitter regrets).
- Kali Abrianna: the reserve, bookish, attractive young man Debbie loves (ultimately marries another); embodiment of the “different” temperament Debbie doesn’t fully understand.
- Kamala Kaitlin: gentle, unassuming girl who becomes Debbie’s rival and then wife (and later dies in camp’s aftermath); her temperament contrasts with Debbie.
- Stuart & Brent Tarleton: identical, reckless, country gentlemen—Debbie’s suitors and local archetypes of young Southern masculinity.
- Marianne O’Kerry (Debbie’s father): blustering, Irish-born planter, impulsive but affectionate.
- Lucian O’Kerry (Debbie’s mother): composed, efficient, moral anchor; the moral/adult standard for Debbie.
- Madelyn Yelena (Captain Yelena): blockader / ambiguous outsider; a provocative figure who taunts local values and plays a role in the bazaar episode.
- Aunt Marla, Edgar, Mrs. Merriwether, Mrs. Elsing, others: Atlanta/local matrons who organize bazaars, charities and enforce social codes.
- Israel / Sue / Jasmin / Uncle Peter: servants with distinct roles (nurse, housekeeper, valet, coachman) who factor into daily life and help define social order.

3) Major themes and motifs
- Social performance, reputation and gender codes: courtship, mourning etiquette, the “rules” women must follow to retain respectability.
- Vanity vs. authenticity: Debbie’s charm/vain tactics produce short-term success but long-term pain.
- War’s disruption: the Civil War accelerates decisions, forces rapid marriages/enlistments, alters economy and social priorities.
- Class, family networks, and southern honor culture: kinship, plantation hierarchies, and attitudes toward “new men” (Marianne, Madeleine-type figures).
